    <h1>Number Grid Puzzle: A Complete Guide to 8 Types You Can Play Free</h1>

    <p>A number grid puzzle is any logic puzzle played on a grid where numbers serve as clues or fill values. The grid turns abstract reasoning into something you can see and touch: every deduction has a cell, every certainty has a coordinate. That is why they are so addictive. Your brain loves writing progress down.</p>

    <p>This guide covers eight distinct types of number grid puzzles, from the world-famous Sudoku to the lesser-known Hitori. Each one has a clear explanation, and several come with free playable examples you can try right in your browser. No download, no sign-up. If you have ten minutes and a curious mind, you can start solving right now.</p>

    <p>Whether you are a complete beginner looking for your first puzzle or a seasoned solver hunting for something new, these eight types represent the full spectrum of what number grids can do: arithmetic, spatial reasoning, picture logic, and pure deduction. Let's go through them one by one.</p>

    <h2>1. Sudoku: The King of Number Grid Puzzles</h2>

    <p>Sudoku is the most recognized number grid puzzle on earth. You get a 9 by 9 grid divided into nine 3 by 3 boxes, and your job is to fill every empty cell with a digit from 1 to 9 so that no digit repeats in any row, column, or box. That is the entire rule set. No math, no addition, just placement logic.</p>

    <p>What makes Sudoku endure is its difficulty scaling. Easy puzzles resolve through simple scanning: look at a row, see what is missing, check the column and box, place the digit. Hard puzzles demand advanced techniques with names like naked pairs, hidden singles, X-wings, and swordfish. The jump from beginner to expert feels like learning a new language using the same alphabet.</p>

    <p>Sudoku was popularized by the Japanese publisher Nikoli in the 1980s and exploded worldwide in 2004 when The Times of London began printing it. Today it appears in hundreds of newspapers, has sold millions of books, and mathematicians have proven that the minimum number of starting clues for a unique solution is exactly 17. If you have only ever played one number grid puzzle, it was almost certainly Sudoku, and the good news is that the other seven on this list will feel like fresh territory built on familiar foundations.</p>

    <h2>2. Shikaku: Rectangle Partition Puzzle</h2>

    <p>Shikaku, whose name means "divide by square" in Japanese, is the rectangle partition puzzle. You get a grid with numbers scattered across it, and your job is to divide the entire grid into rectangles so that each rectangle contains exactly one number, and that number equals the number of cells the rectangle covers. A clue of 6 must own six cells: it could be a 1 by 6, a 2 by 3, or a 3 by 2, and the surrounding clues quietly eliminate all but one option.</p>

    <p>The rules fit in two sentences, but the solving has real depth. Every rectangle you lock in reshapes the possibilities around it, creating cascades of forced moves. Shikaku is part bookkeeping, part mapmaking, and it rewards both careful scanning and a bit of spatial intuition. It first appeared in Nikoli, the same Japanese puzzle publisher that gave the world Sudoku.</p>

