    <h2>3. Akari (Light Up): Place Bulbs, Light the Grid</h2>

    <p>Akari, known outside Japan as Light Up, is the grid puzzle for people who like their deduction with a bit of atmosphere. You get a grid of white and black cells, and you place light bulbs on white cells so that every white cell ends up illuminated. The catch: no two bulbs can shine on each other in the same unobstructed row or column. Black cells block light, and numbered black cells tell you exactly how many bulbs must sit adjacent to them.</p>

    <p>Every placement ripples outward. One bulb lights its row and column and simultaneously forbids a whole set of future placements. The endgame has a lovely payoff: when the last few dark cells can only be lit one way, the whole board resolves in a chain of forced moves. Akari rewards patience and careful scanning, and it pairs beautifully with Shikaku since the two puzzles use similar grids for completely different goals, one dividing space and the other illuminating it.</p>

    <div class="cta-box">
      <p>Or try lighting up the grid: <a href="/akari/">Play Akari free &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>

    <h2>4. Kakuro: Crossword Meets Math</h2>

    <p>Kakuro is what happened when someone looked at a crossword and a math puzzle and decided they belonged together. The grid looks like a crossword, but the entries are digits 1 through 9, and the clues are sums. A clue of 16 across means the digits in that run add up to 16, with no digit repeated within a run.</p>

    <p>That single twist creates beautiful constraints. Only certain digit combinations can make certain sums: a three-cell run summing to 7 must contain 1, 2, and 4 in some order, no exceptions. Experienced solvers memorize these combination tables the way musicians memorize scales. Kakuro is older than Sudoku in its Japanese home, where it has run in newspapers since the 1950s under the name Kasho Kuro.</p>

    <p>Fair warning: Kakuro is less forgiving at the start than Sudoku. The first few solves feel like wading through syrup. But the moment the combination tables click, it becomes one of the most satisfying number grid puzzles there is. If you like your logic with actual arithmetic in it, Kakuro is waiting.</p>

    <h2>5. Nonograms (Picross): Picture Logic Puzzles</h2>

    <p>Nonograms, also sold as Picross or Griddlers, are the picture puzzles of the number grid family. Along the top and left edges of an empty grid you get number clues, and each clue lists the lengths of consecutive filled runs in that row or column, in order. Your task is to fill cells until the whole grid resolves into a small picture, usually something charming like a cat or an umbrella.</p>

    <p>A row of length 10 with the clue "10" fills completely. A clue of "8 1" has eight filled cells, at least one gap, then one more, and working out where the flexibility lies is the heart of the game. Nonograms reward a slightly different muscle than Sudoku: where Sudoku is pure constraint checking, Nonograms mix logic with a running visual hypothesis. You half-see the picture before it is confirmed.</p>

    <p>They are enormously popular on the Nintendo Switch and phones under the Picross name, and a well-made one delivers a small emotional reward at the end that pure number puzzles cannot match: the finished image. If you want your number grid puzzle to surprise you with a picture, Nonograms are the answer.</p>

    <h2>6. KenKen: Arithmetic Cage Puzzles</h2>

    <p>KenKen looks like Sudoku at first glance: fill an N by N grid with digits 1 through N so that no digit repeats in any row or column. The twist is that the grid is divided into irregular cages, each with a target number and an arithmetic operation. The digits in a cage must combine using that operation to hit the target. A two-cell cage labeled "6+" means the two digits add to 6. A cage labeled "3&divide;" means one digit divided by the other equals 3.</p>

    <p>The result is Sudoku's logic plus real arithmetic, and the combination is surprisingly satisfying. Easy KenKen puzzles use small grids with simple addition cages. Hard ones throw division and multiplication into 9 by 9 grids where every cage is an extra constraint on top of the row and column rules. KenKen was invented by Japanese math teacher Tetsuya Miyamoto in 2004 as a teaching tool, and it has since become a staple in classrooms and newspapers worldwide.</p>

    <h2>7. Slitherlink: Draw the Loop</h2>

    <p>Slitherlink is the odd one out on this list, and proudly so. Instead of filling cells, you draw a single continuous loop along the dotted lines between them. Numbered cells tell you how many of their four edges the loop uses: a 3 cell has exactly three of its borders on the loop, a 0 cell has none, and the finished loop must close on itself without ever crossing or touching.</p>

    <p>The loop wanders, doubles back, and teases you with long stretches of ambiguity before a single edge placement suddenly forces a dozen others. Slitherlink has a reputation as the most spatially intuitive of the Nikoli puzzles: solvers often describe feeling the loop's shape before they can prove it, then working backward to justify what their eyes already knew. Start with small grids, treat every 0 as a gift, and never trust a loop that has not yet closed.</p>

    <h2>8. Hitori: Eliminate the Duplicates</h2>

    <p>Hitori, meaning "alone" in Japanese, flips the usual number grid formula on its head. Instead of filling cells, you shade them out. You start with a grid full of numbers, and your job is to shade cells so that no number appears more than once in any row or column, while keeping all unshaded cells connected and never shading two cells that touch orthogonally.</p>

    <p>The paradox of Hitori is that it starts with a complete grid and asks you to make it sparser. Every number you remove ripples through its row and column, potentially forcing other removals or protecting other duplicates from being touched. The connectivity rule adds a spatial dimension that pure elimination puzzles usually lack: you cannot just shade every duplicate, because the survivors have to form one connected group. Hitori is quick to learn, satisfying to solve, and a refreshing change of pace when other number grid puzzles start to feel samey.</p>

